    "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Probability Involving a Squared Random Variable",
    "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=1).$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's first add an ${X}^{2}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third rows of our table, we can immediately conclude that \n\n$$\nP({X}^{2}=1)=0.1+0.2=0.3.\n$$",
